<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;"><strong>After last week=E2=80=99s sharp lo=
sses in the markets</strong> some stock market authorities are rolling out =
support measures to stem the bleeding.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Over the weekend, South Korea=E2=80=99s fi=
nance ministry, central bank, and top regulators agreed to prepare to possi=
bly deploy their market stabilisation fund. They=E2=80=99ve not pulled the =
trigger yet, but it=E2=80=99s a signal to investors that they would do, if =
necessary.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Taiwan is taking action too; its NT$500bn =
(=C2=A311.5bn) National Financial Stabilisation Fund said it has entered th=
e market to support Taiwanese stocks and urged investors to remain confiden=
t.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Taiwanese authorities also widened their c=
urbs on short sales of local shares, making it harder to bet against stocks=
.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">China, meanwhile, is making it easier for =
investors to snap up shares on credit, by lowering the margin requirement f=
or buying securities with borrowed funds. The Shanghai and Shenzhen stock e=
xchanges also introduced a new batch of fee reduction measures, in what sta=
te newswire Xinhua called =E2=80=9Cmeasures to steady=E2=80=9D the stock ma=
rket.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">China=E2=80=99s state-backed funds also po=
ured a record amount of money into the market on Friday, according to Bloom=
berg.</p>
